by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-12T10:41:03Z

Reworked to drop the separate attribute, @MatTheCat's naming point being part of it.

`hidden` already lives on `#[AsCommand]`, and "when does this command show up in `list`" is the same concern, so this is now an option there rather than a second attribute you have to remember to write alongside:

```php
#[AsCommand(name: 'app:debug:dump', listedAt: OutputInterface::VERBOSITY_VERBOSE)]
```

`listedAt` says what it controls without claiming anything about the command's own verbosity, and without borrowing PHP's meaning of "visibility".

The other reason for folding it in: `AddConsoleCommandPass` reads `command`, `description`, `help` and `usages` from the `console.command` tag, so commands registered in YAML or XML get those without attributes. The separate attribute could never reach them. There is now a `listed_at` tag attribute alongside the option, following `description` and `help`.

`hidden` is left alone. Mapping it onto `VERBOSITY_QUIET` worked only because `isHidden()` was still checked separately, and it read backwards: quiet is a lower level, yet it meant "never shown".

@stof, the lazy-loading concern from #64142 is respected: the value is read at compile time and set on both the command definition and the `LazyCommand` wrapper, so `list` still renders without initializing anything.

Console tests pass (Command 131, DependencyInjection 36, Descriptor 147, Application 157). Two tests added, one for the filtering and one for the tag path.

What is left is the feature itself rather than its shape, and it is @MatTheCat's question from #64142 that deserves an answer: commands you use daily are typically run without listing them first, so is a cluttered `list` a real problem worth an option for? @jprivet-dev, a concrete case would help here.

